# The tick cost of one write, and of one read, as a script sees them.
#
# `benchmarks/` measures the plugin's own calls on a bare JVM. This measures what a script pays on a real
# server thread, which is the layer the plan calls for and the one a server owner lives in.
#
# Two numbers come out of every measurement, and both are the script's own:
#
#   * the wall clock around one statement, read from Skript's own `now`, and the ticks the server spent on
#     it, from the counter beside this file. A statement that waits parks the trigger that issued it, so a
#     write cannot resume before the next tick: the tick count is how a script sees that, and the wall
#     clock is quantized by it. Skript writes a timespan as `0.05 seconds`, so the wall clock resolves to
#     10 ms and cannot resolve below it.
#   * the longest tick the statement lived through, from the same counter's gap observer. This is what
#     answers "how much server thread did one write cost": work under a tick's length leaves the gap at a
#     normal tick, while work that overruns one lengthens it. At 10 ms resolution that is what it can see.
#
# What no number here is: an MSPT percentile for the server. The gap observer is a Skript trigger, so it
# sees this run's ticks and carries its own cost; a percentile of tick durations over a server's life needs
# an observer with better manners than a script has, and that would be a second plugin in the server.
#
# Sizes are 100, 500, 1000, 2500, 5000 and 10000 rows in a six-column table, because six columns is the
# shape the 30000-value statement budget was measured on: 5000 rows is exactly one statement and 10000 rows
# is two. The curve is where that boundary is supposed to show, and where the 5000-row read ceiling is
# supposed to refuse. One round is run per firing of this task, so the server is booted fresh for each and
# the spread between runs is visible instead of being averaged away inside one.
#
# Only the key of each row is filled. The suite's own row-ceiling script fills one column for the same
# reason: a row is paid for twice on this side, once when the script builds it and once when the statement
# turns the variable back into values, and five more columns of NULL would pay the second half five more
# times without changing what the statement costs the database.
#
# The six sizes are written out one after another rather than looped over, and every loop below is written
# at the top level of this trigger with a literal count. That is not style: an earlier version of this script
# looped, and what the loop body wrote was dropped. The cause was measured later, in the suite's own server
# test (`elements/32-loop-value.sk`): inside a nested loop the plain `loop-number` and `loop-value` are
# ambiguous, and Skript refuses the *line that reads them* while parsing — "There are multiple loops that
# match loop-number. Use loop-number-1/2/3/etc." — so the body around that line never runs and a batch filled
# there comes out empty. The suffixes count from the outermost loop inward, so `-1` is the outer loop. The
# DIAG line after the first fill keeps this visible, because a fill that produces nothing should say so in
# one line rather than in ninety measurements.
#
# Nothing here deletes, and not for the reason first recorded here. A delete is refused by this dialect
# whether or not a limit is written: the suite's `elements/31-statement-shape.sk` measures a bare
# `delete entities from table "..." and wait`, the same statement with a where block, and the same with
# `with limit 1` — all three come back `Limited delete is not supported by this JDBC dialect.` and claim no
# affected rows, and `update entities` written without a limit is refused the same way. Each write therefore
# owns a range of keys instead, the read that follows asks for that range, and the count the database reports
# is cumulative: the reader turns consecutive counts into differences, which is what says whether the write
# actually wrote.
#
# The setup is written at the top level too. The reason first recorded here — that a `create a connection`
# inside a conditional was never created and reported nothing either — did not reproduce when the suite
# measured it again (`elements/31-statement-shape.sk`: a connection made inside an `if` and used after it
# wrote its row, and an `insert` inside an `if` ran). The setup stays at the top level for the plain reason
# that it happens once, before anything can depend on it, and the guards below stop the trigger and do
# nothing else.
#
# SQLite is the database because it is the one this machine reaches without a container. The two pragmas
# are the suite's: without them the driver syncs the file per row and this would measure the disk.

every 5 seconds:
    # The guards come first and contain nothing but the stop, which is how every script in `server-test`
    # keeps itself to one run.
    if {bench::done::curve} is set:
        stop trigger
    if {bench::started::curve} is set:
        stop trigger
    set {bench::started::curve} to true

    # The plugin and Skript are loaded before any of this runs, so this is only long enough for the tick
    # counter to have something in it.
    wait 5 seconds

    create a connection named "bench" to database "JDBC" with properties:
        driver: "org.sqlite.JDBC"
        # The same pragmas the suite's row-ceiling script uses, for the same reason.
        url: "jdbc:sqlite:plugins/Skript/bench-probe.db?synchronous=OFF&journal_mode=MEMORY"
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve connect: %last database error%"

    in connection "bench":
        register a database table "orm_bench_probe":
            id: bigint, primary key, not null
            pad1: int, nullable
            pad2: int, nullable
            pad3: int, nullable
            pad4: int, nullable
            pad5: int, nullable
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve register table: %last database error%"

    # The key ranges are handed out rather than reset, because nothing here deletes.
    set {bench::base} to 0

    # 100 rows, which is one statement well inside every budget.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 100 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=DIAG first batch first row is [%{bench::batch::1::id}%] of range above %{bench::base}%"

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve write 100: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    # The timespans are bracketed because Skript writes them as "0.05 seconds": a reader that split the line
    # on spaces would read the unit as the next field rather than as part of the value.
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH write: rows=100 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 100 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH read: rows=100 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    # 500 rows.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 500 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve write 500: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH write: rows=500 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 500 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH read: rows=500 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    # 1000 rows.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 1000 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve write 1000: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH write: rows=1000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 1000 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH read: rows=1000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    # 2500 rows.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 2500 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve write 2500: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH write: rows=2500 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 2500 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH read: rows=2500 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    # 5000 rows: the write budget in one statement, and the read ceiling exactly reached.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 5000 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve write 5000: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH write: rows=5000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 5000 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH read: rows=5000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    # 10000 rows: twice the write budget, so two statements, and twice the read ceiling, so a refusal that
    # is the ceiling being re-derived rather than restated.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 10000 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve write 10000: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH write: rows=10000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 10000 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH read: rows=10000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    # Controls, before the warm pass. Every read above left the longest tick at exactly a tick's own length,
    # while the reading page says a 5000-row read of six columns — 30 000 values, the size at which the
    # result tree is attached rather than written value by value — spends about 36 milliseconds of the
    # server thread. Both cannot be true, and more reads cannot settle it: what settles it is a block of
    # work whose size this script chooses instead of measures. A spin is a counted loop with no statement
    # and no wait inside it, so it runs to completion on the server thread and its own duration is what
    # `spin=` says. If the observer reports an overhang of that size, it can see work of that size, and the
    # reads above are genuinely not lengthening a tick; if it reports nothing, the instrument is what is
    # short. `spun=` is how many times the loop ran, because a loop the parser quietly dropped would
    # otherwise read as a block that was fast rather than as one that never happened.
    #
    # The idle control is the floor: six seconds of waiting with nothing else watched must report a longest
    # tick of exactly one tick, or the observer is adding something of its own to every reading.
    #
    # The sizes were wrong the first time and the run said so. That version used 5000, 20000, 60000 and
    # 150000 iterations, which measured 10, 10, 20 and 50 ms: an iteration costs about a third of a
    # microsecond, so the largest block was one tick long exactly, and a block that fits inside a tick
    # leaves the tick un-lengthened no matter how well the observer works. Nothing was learned from it
    # about whether the observer can see an overhang at all. These sizes are past a tick on purpose —
    # about 40, 60, 100 and 300 ms — so that the smallest one is the size the reading page describes and
    # the largest one cannot fit in any tick.
    #
    # What those sizes measured, for whoever reads this next: 120000 and 300000 iterations both took 90 ms
    # and came back as 90 ms ticks, 180000 took 50 ms and came back as 60, 900000 took 290 ms and came back
    # as 300 — 250 ms of overhang — and the idle window came back as 70. So the observer does see an overhang
    # once there is one, and its floor is a tick: half a tick of block reads as 0 to 10 ms of overhang, and
    # less than that reads as nothing at all, however long it actually ran.
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    set {bench::before} to now
    wait 6 seconds
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH calibrate: label=idle6s ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% window=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] spun=0"

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::spin::before} to now
    set {_spun} to 0
    loop 120000 times:
        set {_spun} to {_spun} + 1
    set {bench::spin::elapsed} to difference between {bench::spin::before} and now
    wait 1 second
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH calibrate: label=spin120000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% spin=[%{bench::spin::elapsed}%] window=[%difference between {bench::spin::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] spun=%{_spun}%"

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::spin::before} to now
    set {_spun} to 0
    loop 180000 times:
        set {_spun} to {_spun} + 1
    set {bench::spin::elapsed} to difference between {bench::spin::before} and now
    wait 1 second
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH calibrate: label=spin180000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% spin=[%{bench::spin::elapsed}%] window=[%difference between {bench::spin::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] spun=%{_spun}%"

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::spin::before} to now
    set {_spun} to 0
    loop 300000 times:
        set {_spun} to {_spun} + 1
    set {bench::spin::elapsed} to difference between {bench::spin::before} and now
    wait 1 second
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH calibrate: label=spin300000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% spin=[%{bench::spin::elapsed}%] window=[%difference between {bench::spin::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] spun=%{_spun}%"

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::spin::before} to now
    set {_spun} to 0
    loop 900000 times:
        set {_spun} to {_spun} + 1
    set {bench::spin::elapsed} to difference between {bench::spin::before} and now
    wait 1 second
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH calibrate: label=spin900000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% spin=[%{bench::spin::elapsed}%] window=[%difference between {bench::spin::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] spun=%{_spun}%"

    # The 5000-row write again, at the end and with everything warm. Every number above is the first time
    # that statement ran in this server, and the first 5000-row write measured 80 ms of tick overrun where
    # the library's own benchmark reports about 10 ms for the same call on a bare JVM. Repeating one size
    # with the JVM warm says whether that gap is this server's first pass or its steady state, and the size
    # is the one the write budget and the read ceiling both land on, so the warm read is the other half of
    # the same comparison. It has its own name in the log rather than joining the size above, so nothing
    # about it is averaged into the curve.
    add 100000 to {bench::base}
    delete {bench::batch::*}
    loop 5000 times:
        set {bench::batch::%loop-number%::id} to {bench::base} + loop-number

    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        insert many {bench::batch::*} into table "orm_bench_probe" and store affected rows in {_affected} and wait
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    if last database error is set:
        log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH=FAIL curve warm write 5000: %last database error%"
    in connection "bench":
        execute query "SELECT COUNT(*) AS total FROM orm_bench_probe" and store the result in {bench::count::*}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH warmwrite: rows=5000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] affected=%{_affected}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}%"

    delete {bench::rows::*}
    set {bench::tick::before} to {bench::tick}
    delete {bench::tick::gap::max}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to true
    set {bench::before} to now
    in connection "bench":
        select many entities from table "orm_bench_probe" and store the results in {bench::rows::*}:
            where all:
                id > {bench::base}
    set {bench::tick::watch} to false
    set {bench::stored} to 0
    loop 5000 times:
        if {bench::rows::%loop-number%::id} is set:
            add 1 to {bench::stored}
    log "SKRIPTORM_BENCH warmread: rows=5000 ticks=%{bench::tick} - {bench::tick::before}% ms=[%difference between {bench::before} and now%] gap=[%{bench::tick::gap::max}%] stored=%{bench::stored}% total=%{bench::count::1::total}% error=%last database error%"
    delete {bench::rows::*}

    set {bench::done::curve} to true
